			<div class="content" id="post-content-4751109">
									

<p><em>PM request acknowledged.</em></p>
<hr />
<p>T.L. at your service!</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Here&nbsp;lies&nbsp;crit&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Bye&nbsp;bye&nbsp;crit&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'm personally not too fond of this concept. To me, this just sorta comes off as <em>Thomas and Friends</em>, but with trucks. I guess if you both focus on really incorporating the action thriller, and maybe a bit of comedy into the narrative, it could mask a bit of that reminiscence.</p>
<p>Another concern I have with this is that I feel like you both haven't really elaborated enough with the narrative. I personally would like to see a bit more context from what is already presented. Right now, it just doesn't seem like enough for me to provide an actual full opinion on it.</p>
<p>With all that said, I'm gonna second cybersqyd's suggestion on a tale for this. I feel like this won't stand very well on it's own just as a SCP article.</p>
<p>Overall, I also would be unwilling to greenlight this as it is. There isn't enough to convince me that this isn't just a <em>Thomas and Friends</em> type of concept. Really elaborate on the action thriller when fleshing out the narrative.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
If you have any questions regarding my critique, please let me know. You are welcome to PM me for another look, or you may follow up with someone from the Butterfly Squad Roster or IRC Chat rooms.
